1
00:00:00,533 --> 00:00:02,233
Many people read Buffett

2
00:00:02,533 --> 00:00:04,100
but the easiest place to misread him

3
00:00:04,100 --> 00:00:06,666
is treating him as someone who's just great at making money

4
00:00:06,766 --> 00:00:08,566
as if one glance at a company

5
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:10,633
tells him which stock will rise

6
00:00:10,833 --> 00:00:13,333
as if ordinary people just need to learn a few of his lines

7
00:00:13,400 --> 00:00:15,533
to catch the next tenbagger

8
00:00:15,566 --> 00:00:16,533
But I think

9
00:00:16,600 --> 00:00:18,433
what's truly powerful about Buffett

10
00:00:18,566 --> 00:00:20,466
isn't how many opportunities he sees through

11
00:00:20,533 --> 00:00:23,000
but how many he's willing to let go when he doesn't understand them

12
00:00:23,100 --> 00:00:24,733
This goes against common sense

13
00:00:24,833 --> 00:00:27,233
because we assume making money comes from action

14
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:28,166
from vision

15
00:00:28,200 --> 00:00:29,433
from jumping in on time

16
00:00:29,966 --> 00:00:31,333
But most ordinary people lose money

17
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:32,933
not because they didn't act

18
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:35,833
but because they got pulled into someone else's opportunity too easily

19
00:00:35,933 --> 00:00:38,066
At a dinner someone says this project is a sure thing

20
00:00:38,233 --> 00:00:40,200
in a group chat someone says this coin is about to moon

21
00:00:40,366 --> 00:00:42,033
on your feed someone posts their gains

22
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:43,566
You don't really understand it

23
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:44,800
but you're afraid of missing out

24
00:00:44,900 --> 00:00:46,600
so you put your money in

25
00:00:46,600 --> 00:00:48,533
and hand over your judgment along with it

26
00:00:48,700 --> 00:00:51,033
This video isn't about how to become Buffett

27
00:00:51,166 --> 00:00:52,833
or about which stock to buy

28
00:00:53,033 --> 00:00:55,233
just one judgment ordinary people can use

29
00:00:55,366 --> 00:00:56,666
what truly protects you

30
00:00:56,700 --> 00:00:58,233
isn't catching every opportunity

31
00:00:58,333 --> 00:01:00,233
but knowing which money you shouldn't touch

32
00:01:00,366 --> 00:01:02,066
Picture a scene first

33
00:01:02,433 --> 00:01:04,400
After work you're sitting at a dinner table

34
00:01:04,633 --> 00:01:07,000
and a friend next to you suddenly lowers his voice

35
00:01:07,300 --> 00:01:09,233
saying he's got an inside opportunity lately

36
00:01:09,366 --> 00:01:10,500
a new project

37
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:11,666
a new fund

38
00:01:11,733 --> 00:01:12,866
a new sector

39
00:01:13,100 --> 00:01:14,200
He talks fast

40
00:01:14,366 --> 00:01:15,533
and the words sound beautiful

41
00:01:15,533 --> 00:01:16,233
valuation

42
00:01:16,233 --> 00:01:17,000
cycle

43
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:17,933
window

44
00:01:17,933 --> 00:01:19,000
get in early

45
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:20,533
You don't quite follow

46
00:01:20,700 --> 00:01:22,266
but someone at the table is nodding

47
00:01:22,366 --> 00:01:23,766
someone has already wired money

48
00:01:24,033 --> 00:01:26,333
someone says if you don't get in now it'll be too late

49
00:01:26,633 --> 00:01:28,166
The hardest feeling in that moment

50
00:01:28,166 --> 00:01:28,966
isn't greed

51
00:01:29,100 --> 00:01:30,866
it's the fear of looking left behind

52
00:01:31,166 --> 00:01:32,533
You're afraid everyone else gets it

53
00:01:32,533 --> 00:01:33,633
and you're the only one who doesn't

54
00:01:33,800 --> 00:01:35,000
So many losses

55
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:36,833
don't start from greed

56
00:01:36,900 --> 00:01:39,833
they start from being too embarrassed to admit you don't understand

57
00:01:39,933 --> 00:01:42,433
Buffett rarely pretends to understand in those moments

58
00:01:42,566 --> 00:01:44,333
He has a very plain saying

59
00:01:44,433 --> 00:01:46,033
anything outside my circle of competence

60
00:01:46,100 --> 00:01:46,900
I don't touch

61
00:01:47,033 --> 00:01:48,633
It sounds too conservative

62
00:01:48,633 --> 00:01:49,733
even a bit dumb

63
00:01:49,833 --> 00:01:51,333
but think about it carefully

64
00:01:51,433 --> 00:01:54,733
someone who for decades avoids what he doesn't understand

65
00:01:54,966 --> 00:01:55,933
that's not dumb

66
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:57,966
that's extreme self-control

67
00:01:58,166 --> 00:01:59,966
The mistake ordinary people make most easily

68
00:01:59,966 --> 00:02:01,233
is mistaking someone else's certainty

69
00:02:01,233 --> 00:02:03,033
for their own certainty

70
00:02:03,233 --> 00:02:04,600
someone else speaking with confidence

71
00:02:04,600 --> 00:02:06,033
doesn't mean you really understand it

72
00:02:06,133 --> 00:02:07,366
someone else making money

73
00:02:07,366 --> 00:02:09,466
doesn't mean you'll be able to get out once you're in

74
00:02:09,633 --> 00:02:11,233
someone else being able to absorb losses

75
00:02:11,233 --> 00:02:13,366
doesn't mean you'll still sleep at night when you lose

76
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:15,000
The most expensive part of an opportunity

77
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:16,566
isn't the money you put in

78
00:02:16,733 --> 00:02:18,966
it's that it makes you fake understanding

79
00:02:18,966 --> 00:02:20,766
when you don't really understand

80
00:02:20,766 --> 00:02:22,266
once you start faking

81
00:02:22,300 --> 00:02:24,666
every step after that becomes harder to undo

82
00:02:24,900 --> 00:02:26,566
Many people hear circle of competence

83
00:02:26,833 --> 00:02:28,666
and think it means limiting themselves

84
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:30,400
as if admitting I don't understand

85
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:31,966
equals admitting I'm no good

86
00:02:32,233 --> 00:02:34,800
but the real meaning of the circle isn't self-doubt

87
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:38,666
it's knowing where you can make reliable judgments

88
00:02:38,766 --> 00:02:40,633
You've worked in your industry for years

89
00:02:40,633 --> 00:02:42,400
you know which bosses are reliable

90
00:02:42,566 --> 00:02:44,333
which clients will delay payment

91
00:02:44,533 --> 00:02:46,833
you know what signals usually appear

92
00:02:46,933 --> 00:02:49,166
before a hot project falls apart

93
00:02:49,333 --> 00:02:50,600
That is your circle

94
00:02:50,766 --> 00:02:52,166
You take care of your kid every day

95
00:02:52,300 --> 00:02:54,633
you know whether a tutoring class actually cares

96
00:02:54,733 --> 00:02:56,400
or just sells anxiety

97
00:02:56,400 --> 00:02:58,400
you know whether your kid really likes it

98
00:02:58,433 --> 00:03:00,533
or is just pushed by ad scripts

99
00:03:00,633 --> 00:03:01,966
That is also your circle

100
00:03:01,966 --> 00:03:04,333
Circle of competence isn't a finance-only term

101
00:03:04,500 --> 00:03:07,433
it just means the areas where you have a real feel for reality

102
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:08,600
Buffett is powerful

103
00:03:08,600 --> 00:03:10,600
not because his circle is infinitely large

104
00:03:10,633 --> 00:03:13,966
but because he knows exactly where the edge of his circle is

105
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:15,600
The most dangerous moment for a person

106
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:17,200
isn't total ignorance

107
00:03:17,300 --> 00:03:19,466
totally ignorant people are actually cautious

108
00:03:19,633 --> 00:03:21,166
The most dangerous thing is half-understanding

109
00:03:21,200 --> 00:03:22,333
having heard a few terms

110
00:03:22,433 --> 00:03:23,733
having read a few articles

111
00:03:23,800 --> 00:03:25,333
seeing someone around you make money

112
00:03:25,400 --> 00:03:27,533
and then feeling you basically get it

113
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:29,533
Half-understanding makes you bolder

114
00:03:29,633 --> 00:03:31,400
but your capacity to absorb losses doesn't grow

115
00:03:31,400 --> 00:03:33,600
It's like you've only taken a few swimming lessons

116
00:03:33,633 --> 00:03:35,766
but think you can handle the deep end

117
00:03:35,833 --> 00:03:37,166
People on the shore shout to you

118
00:03:37,166 --> 00:03:38,333
the view here is great

119
00:03:38,333 --> 00:03:39,233
the water isn't deep

120
00:03:39,366 --> 00:03:41,066
but you're the one who chokes

121
00:03:41,100 --> 00:03:42,400
The value of the circle

122
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:44,466
is to remind you before you get in

123
00:03:44,500 --> 00:03:46,566
whether you can actually swim these waters

124
00:03:46,633 --> 00:03:48,066
Many ordinary people's problem

125
00:03:48,100 --> 00:03:49,233
isn't having no opportunities

126
00:03:49,366 --> 00:03:51,966
it's treating every opportunity as the same kind of thing

127
00:03:52,300 --> 00:03:54,366
seeing someone make money on short videos

128
00:03:54,533 --> 00:03:56,133
and thinking you should do it too

129
00:03:56,133 --> 00:03:57,933
seeing someone make money on houses

130
00:03:58,000 --> 00:03:59,600
and thinking you should buy too

131
00:03:59,600 --> 00:04:01,766
seeing someone make money on AI tools

132
00:04:01,900 --> 00:04:04,166
and thinking if you don't get in now you're done

133
00:04:04,166 --> 00:04:05,633
But first ask yourself

134
00:04:05,733 --> 00:04:07,433
the judgment this opportunity needs

135
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:08,600
do I actually have it

136
00:04:08,633 --> 00:04:10,400
do I know how it makes money

137
00:04:10,400 --> 00:04:12,133
do I know how it loses money

138
00:04:12,133 --> 00:04:14,166
do I know when to exit

139
00:04:14,166 --> 00:04:16,366
If you can't answer these three questions

140
00:04:16,433 --> 00:04:17,800
then it's not an opportunity

141
00:04:17,833 --> 00:04:20,366
it's just an exam dressed up as an opportunity

142
00:04:20,400 --> 00:04:22,233
and you haven't even read the questions

143
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:23,866
What do ordinary people fear most

144
00:04:24,333 --> 00:04:25,333
Many will say

145
00:04:25,766 --> 00:04:26,633
fear of missing out

146
00:04:26,933 --> 00:04:29,033
fear others get rich while they stay in place

147
00:04:29,333 --> 00:04:30,966
fear the train of the era leaves without them

148
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:32,800
fear everyone around understands more than they do

149
00:04:33,133 --> 00:04:34,733
This fear is real

150
00:04:34,800 --> 00:04:36,433
but it's also the easiest to exploit

151
00:04:36,733 --> 00:04:37,900
Because once you believe

152
00:04:37,900 --> 00:04:39,433
that if you don't get in now it's too late

153
00:04:39,433 --> 00:04:41,433
you'll skip a lot of judgment

154
00:04:41,500 --> 00:04:43,433
you stop asking how this thing makes money

155
00:04:43,566 --> 00:04:45,000
you only ask if you can still get in

156
00:04:45,133 --> 00:04:46,733
you stop asking if you understand it

157
00:04:46,733 --> 00:04:48,400
you only ask how much others put in

158
00:04:48,433 --> 00:04:50,133
you stop asking what the worst case is

159
00:04:50,133 --> 00:04:51,966
you only look at how much you could make

160
00:04:52,033 --> 00:04:54,233
By then you've entered someone else's game

161
00:04:54,300 --> 00:04:56,133
and the rules aren't written by you

162
00:04:56,300 --> 00:04:58,800
Many think Buffett's investing is mysterious

163
00:04:58,900 --> 00:05:01,133
but what he does is actually very plain

164
00:05:01,233 --> 00:05:04,133
he tries to bet only where he can understand the rules

165
00:05:04,133 --> 00:05:04,733
if he doesn't understand

166
00:05:04,733 --> 00:05:05,733
he doesn't bet

167
00:05:05,900 --> 00:05:06,700
This is hard

168
00:05:06,800 --> 00:05:09,266
because not betting makes you look like you have no story

169
00:05:09,400 --> 00:05:11,166
others have new opportunities every day

170
00:05:11,166 --> 00:05:12,100
while you sit there

171
00:05:12,100 --> 00:05:13,400
as if nothing is happening

172
00:05:13,533 --> 00:05:14,900
others post their gains screenshots

173
00:05:14,900 --> 00:05:16,266
while you're still looking at cash flow

174
00:05:16,333 --> 00:05:17,366
others talk about trends

175
00:05:17,366 --> 00:05:19,633
while you're still asking what this company actually sells

176
00:05:19,700 --> 00:05:20,433
This kind of slow

177
00:05:20,433 --> 00:05:22,066
doesn't fit social media

178
00:05:22,200 --> 00:05:24,733
but it fits protecting an ordinary person's capital

179
00:05:24,800 --> 00:05:25,666
You should know

180
00:05:25,766 --> 00:05:28,200
an ordinary person's money isn't like big fund money

181
00:05:28,333 --> 00:05:29,333
if you lose a hundred thousand

182
00:05:29,333 --> 00:05:31,200
it's not a number vanishing from a spreadsheet

183
00:05:31,233 --> 00:05:32,800
it could be years of savings

184
00:05:32,933 --> 00:05:34,366
your parents' medical money

185
00:05:34,400 --> 00:05:35,533
your kid's tuition

186
00:05:35,566 --> 00:05:37,533
your buffer for the next time you switch jobs

187
00:05:37,733 --> 00:05:39,933
So ordinary people can't just look at returns

188
00:05:39,966 --> 00:05:42,233
they also have to look at whether this money is their fallback

189
00:05:42,366 --> 00:05:43,266
fallback money

190
00:05:43,333 --> 00:05:45,600
shouldn't be used to prove you're keeping up with the times

191
00:05:45,833 --> 00:05:47,333
The most mature thing about a person

192
00:05:47,333 --> 00:05:48,766
isn't finally daring to take risks

193
00:05:48,833 --> 00:05:50,800
but finally no longer touching things you don't understand

194
00:05:50,800 --> 00:05:52,833
just to look smart

195
00:05:53,433 --> 00:05:55,966
So how can ordinary people use this Buffett judgment

196
00:05:55,966 --> 00:05:56,700
First

197
00:05:56,933 --> 00:05:58,766
write a list of things you understand

198
00:05:59,166 --> 00:06:00,933
not things you're interested in

199
00:06:01,233 --> 00:06:03,566
but things you actually have judgment on

200
00:06:03,966 --> 00:06:05,500
which industry have you worked in

201
00:06:05,500 --> 00:06:06,800
which pitfalls have you seen

202
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:09,400
which hidden costs do you know others don't

203
00:06:09,600 --> 00:06:11,233
can you explain in plain words

204
00:06:11,233 --> 00:06:12,933
how it actually makes money

205
00:06:13,233 --> 00:06:14,333
if you can't

206
00:06:14,333 --> 00:06:15,466
don't rush to invest

207
00:06:15,800 --> 00:06:16,600
Second

208
00:06:16,600 --> 00:06:18,200
write a do-not-touch list

209
00:06:18,433 --> 00:06:20,633
anything that only makes sense with insider info

210
00:06:20,633 --> 00:06:21,466
don't touch

211
00:06:21,700 --> 00:06:23,333
anything that only talks about future imagination

212
00:06:23,333 --> 00:06:24,833
but not present cash flow

213
00:06:24,833 --> 00:06:25,733
don't touch

214
00:06:25,933 --> 00:06:27,633
anything that requires you to stare at the screen

215
00:06:27,633 --> 00:06:28,833
to feel safe

216
00:06:28,833 --> 00:06:29,733
don't touch

217
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:32,366
anything that makes you embarrassed to admit you don't understand

218
00:06:32,366 --> 00:06:33,600
definitely don't touch

219
00:06:33,933 --> 00:06:36,766
because if an opportunity requires you to fake being smart

220
00:06:36,800 --> 00:06:38,833
it will most likely charge you tuition

221
00:06:38,933 --> 00:06:39,733
Third

222
00:06:39,766 --> 00:06:41,466
change the question from can I make it

223
00:06:41,500 --> 00:06:43,133
to why is it coming to me

224
00:06:43,300 --> 00:06:44,533
This question is harsh

225
00:06:44,533 --> 00:06:45,533
but very useful

226
00:06:45,733 --> 00:06:46,733
A good opportunity

227
00:06:46,733 --> 00:06:48,433
why does it land in front of me

228
00:06:48,733 --> 00:06:50,933
is it because I have judgment others don't

229
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:53,633
or because I'm just the last batch of bag holders

230
00:06:53,833 --> 00:06:55,600
is it because I truly understand it

231
00:06:55,733 --> 00:06:58,600
or because emotions happen to push me to the door

232
00:06:58,833 --> 00:07:00,233
When you start asking this way

233
00:07:00,400 --> 00:07:02,366
a lot of hype slowly cools down

234
00:07:02,600 --> 00:07:04,233
you may not get rich right away

235
00:07:04,300 --> 00:07:06,666
but you'll lose a lot less money you shouldn't have lost

236
00:07:06,800 --> 00:07:08,133
For ordinary people

237
00:07:08,233 --> 00:07:08,966
losing less

238
00:07:08,966 --> 00:07:10,866
sometimes matters more than making more

239
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:13,433
because losing less preserves your options

240
00:07:13,700 --> 00:07:15,800
preserves nights you can sleep through

241
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:18,833
preserves the next time a real opportunity shows up

242
00:07:18,900 --> 00:07:19,933
you still have money

243
00:07:19,966 --> 00:07:20,933
still have mental energy

244
00:07:20,933 --> 00:07:21,933
still can act

245
00:07:22,333 --> 00:07:24,533
The most worth-learning thing about Buffett for ordinary people

246
00:07:24,566 --> 00:07:25,966
isn't the wealth myth

247
00:07:26,100 --> 00:07:28,033
but a very calm kind of refusal

248
00:07:28,200 --> 00:07:28,966
I don't understand

249
00:07:28,966 --> 00:07:29,766
I don't touch

250
00:07:29,966 --> 00:07:31,000
I can miss out

251
00:07:31,033 --> 00:07:33,066
but I won't hand my fallback to hype

252
00:07:33,200 --> 00:07:34,800
This line doesn't sound inspiring

253
00:07:34,833 --> 00:07:35,966
but it feels solid

254
00:07:36,100 --> 00:07:37,366
The truly powerful person

255
00:07:37,400 --> 00:07:39,400
isn't the one standing on every trend

256
00:07:39,566 --> 00:07:41,466
but the one who when the wind is loudest

257
00:07:41,566 --> 00:07:43,733
still knows whether to reach out

258
00:07:43,766 --> 00:07:44,833
There's another judgment

259
00:07:44,833 --> 00:07:46,333
that suits ordinary people especially well

260
00:07:46,433 --> 00:07:48,833
Don't mix up learning with betting

261
00:07:48,900 --> 00:07:50,600
You can research an industry

262
00:07:50,700 --> 00:07:52,600
listen to others talk about new opportunities

263
00:07:52,700 --> 00:07:53,833
read financial reports

264
00:07:53,833 --> 00:07:55,033
learn about AI

265
00:07:55,166 --> 00:07:56,566
study real estate

266
00:07:56,633 --> 00:07:57,933
look into funds

267
00:07:58,033 --> 00:07:59,266
all of these are fine

268
00:07:59,566 --> 00:08:01,166
Learning isn't betting

269
00:08:01,333 --> 00:08:02,633
Many people the moment they learn something

270
00:08:02,700 --> 00:08:04,633
rush to prove they've mastered it

271
00:08:04,766 --> 00:08:05,800
just understood a little

272
00:08:05,800 --> 00:08:06,933
want to buy a little

273
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:08,333
just grasped a concept

274
00:08:08,333 --> 00:08:09,666
want to try it once

275
00:08:09,800 --> 00:08:11,133
This is actually dangerous

276
00:08:11,300 --> 00:08:13,833
because the market doesn't go easy on you

277
00:08:13,833 --> 00:08:15,266
just because you've just started learning

278
00:08:15,366 --> 00:08:16,333
it won't say

279
00:08:16,366 --> 00:08:17,600
this one's a newbie

280
00:08:17,600 --> 00:08:18,966
go easy on him

281
00:08:19,033 --> 00:08:21,400
So allow yourself to stay on the sidelines for a long time

282
00:08:21,633 --> 00:08:23,000
Spectating isn't cowardice

283
00:08:23,100 --> 00:08:26,066
it's the lowest-cost way to learn

284
00:08:26,200 --> 00:08:28,633
You can use a paper account to record your calls

285
00:08:28,766 --> 00:08:30,033
if I bought today

286
00:08:30,033 --> 00:08:31,166
what would my reason be

287
00:08:31,200 --> 00:08:32,433
check again in three months

288
00:08:32,433 --> 00:08:33,766
whether that reason held up

289
00:08:33,933 --> 00:08:35,966
You can track an industry for half a year

290
00:08:36,100 --> 00:08:37,900
see who's really making money in it

291
00:08:37,900 --> 00:08:39,033
who's losing money

292
00:08:39,200 --> 00:08:40,366
who's selling stories

293
00:08:40,366 --> 00:08:41,600
who's selling products

294
00:08:41,633 --> 00:08:42,633
These observations

295
00:08:42,733 --> 00:08:44,866
will slowly expand your circle

296
00:08:45,100 --> 00:08:46,600
but before it expands

297
00:08:46,733 --> 00:08:49,266
don't rush to use real money to prove yourself

298
00:08:49,300 --> 00:08:51,366
The best growth pace for ordinary people

299
00:08:51,400 --> 00:08:52,833
isn't losing while learning

300
00:08:52,966 --> 00:08:54,533
but learning at low cost first

301
00:08:54,733 --> 00:08:56,600
and once your judgment has real feel

302
00:08:56,766 --> 00:08:57,866
try in small steps

303
00:08:57,900 --> 00:08:59,533
Starting with small money

304
00:08:59,533 --> 00:09:01,200
isn't because small money doesn't matter

305
00:09:01,200 --> 00:09:03,733
but because small money exposes your emotions

306
00:09:03,800 --> 00:09:05,100
will you chase rallies

307
00:09:05,100 --> 00:09:06,233
will you panic

308
00:09:06,333 --> 00:09:08,566
will you waver at one comment from someone else

309
00:09:08,633 --> 00:09:10,633
will you want to win it back after a small loss

310
00:09:10,633 --> 00:09:13,600
if you can't control these emotions even with small money

311
00:09:13,633 --> 00:09:15,800
with big money it'll only get uglier

312
00:09:15,900 --> 00:09:17,200
Buffett's patience

313
00:09:17,300 --> 00:09:18,833
isn't sitting there doing nothing

314
00:09:18,966 --> 00:09:21,266
it's his willingness to wait until he truly understands

315
00:09:21,333 --> 00:09:22,466
and his willingness to admit

316
00:09:22,566 --> 00:09:24,166
that most of the noise in the world

317
00:09:24,166 --> 00:09:25,600
actually has nothing to do with him

318
00:09:25,766 --> 00:09:27,766
This line also applies to small accounts

319
00:09:27,900 --> 00:09:29,600
you don't have to chase every trend

320
00:09:29,700 --> 00:09:31,433
or squeeze into every track

321
00:09:31,533 --> 00:09:33,166
first know what you talk about

322
00:09:33,166 --> 00:09:34,166
what you can talk about

323
00:09:34,166 --> 00:09:35,633
why users trust you

324
00:09:35,633 --> 00:09:37,833
that's the circle of competence for content

325
00:09:38,033 --> 00:09:39,633
seeing your own plate clearly

326
00:09:39,633 --> 00:09:41,866
matters more than staring at other people's hits

327
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:44,166
Next episode we talk about Fan Jin passing the imperial exam

328
00:09:44,233 --> 00:09:45,966
many people treat him as a joke

329
00:09:45,966 --> 00:09:47,833
but the truly scary part

330
00:09:47,900 --> 00:09:50,933
is a person betting his whole life on a single ticket
